  "Religious & Moral Education JHS2 s5 ss2 - Substance Abuse and Decent Living": PracticeModel(
    audioPath: '',
    questions: [
      PracticeQuestion(
        scenario: "Question 1: (a) Explain four reasons why students abuse drugs.\n"
            "(b) State three effects of substance abuse.\n"
            "(BECE 2005 Q4)",
        answer: "Correct Answer: (a) Answer:\n"
            "⦿ Refusal to stick to medical advice: Some students refuse to seek the advice of a qualified medical practitioner in order that they may do self-medication.\n"
            "⦿ Emotional stress situation: Students abuse drugs so that they can control their emotions such as shyness, anger, etc.\n"
            "⦿ Ignorance: Students abuse drugs out of ignorance or not knowing the negative effects of drug abuse.\n"
            "⦿ Peer influence: Students abuse drugs due to peer pressure or seeing their friends in engaging in such activities.\n"
            "⦿ Economic stress situation: Situations such as unemployment and poverty may encourage students to abuse drugs so that they can forget their present problems.\n"
            "⦿ Broken homes: The absence of parents in the home to guide and counsel students can lead to students abusing drugs.\n"
            "⦿ For high academic achievement: Students sometimes think that certain drugs can help them to stay awake and study late into the night so that they can perform well in the exams.\n"
            "⦿ Lack of recreational activities and facilities can also lead to drug abuse.\n"
            "⦿ Lack of self-control or self-discipline: Some students abuse drugs simply because they are not able to control their desires.\n"
            "⦿ Rebellion against authority: Students resort to illegal drugs to enable them become bold and fearless so that they may rebel against authorities.\n"
            "⦿ Advertisements: Advertisements on radio, television and other media encourage students to buy and use the drugs without medical approval.\n"
            "⦿ Curiosity: The tendency to abuse drugs may also be due to the desire to experience its side effects.\n"
            "⦿ Enhancing their self-esteem or image.\n\n"
            "Answer: (b)\n"
            "⦿ Loss of life or severe clinical health problems: The abuse of substances such as marijuana and other illegal drugs can cause the user to lose his or her life (death). That is, it can make the user suffer from stroke, heart beat or cancer, lung cancer, etc. leading to his or her death.\n"
            "⦿ Reduced workplace productivity and high absenteeism: The psychological problems associated with abuse of substances such as marijuana, cocaine, etc. reduce the ability of the users to perform efficiently at workplaces. This leads to absenteeism or low output in the country.\n"
            "⦿ Propensity to engage in social vices: The abuse of substances can have negative impact on the users to engage in social vices such as theft, rebellion against authority, rape, etc.\n"
            "⦿ Organ damage and mental challenges: It may also lead to health problems. That is, people who use substances such as tramadol, marijuana, cocaine, etc. can suffer from heart problems, cancer and mental challenges.\n"
            "⦿ Brain damage and physical deformaties: The use of illegal drugs such as cocaine, marijuana, tramadol, etc. can deform a person or cause physical damage to the body of a person. For instance, it can damage the brain of a person leading to lapses in his or her memory or madness.\n"
            "⦿ Dependency and addiction: Abuse of substances such as marijuana, tramadol, alcohol, etc. may cause the user to become dependent and addicted to the drug. This makes such people find it difficult to stop using such drugs.\n"
            "⦿ Delayed medical treatment: It can cause delay in the proper treatment of a disease. That is, it can make health personnel find it difficult to administer proper medical treatment to the users when the need arises and as such has to delay the health delivery process.\n"
            "⦿ Extreme mood swings and abnormal behaviour: The use of substances such as marijuana, cocaine, excessive alcohol, etc. can cause mood swings and madness or abnormal behaviour of the users.\n"
            "⦿ Loss of social respect, dignity, and personal opportunities: It can lead to loss of respect, dignity and opportunities in life. That is, it can make people have negative perceptions about the users which can cause them lose respect of people in the society. This can therefore cause them to lose opportunities such as jobs, suitors, school admission, friends, etc.\n"
            "⦿ Casual sex and sexual crimes: It can make the users become addicted and cause them engage in casual sex and rape.\n"
            "⦿ Hallucinations and false confidence: It also causes the users to get false confidence or hallucination and this can make them engage in unacceptable lifestyles such as violence, rape, armed robbery, etc. in the country. It can also lead them to commit murder in the country.\n"
            "⦿ Reproductive health complications: Abuse of substances such as alcohol, drugs, etc. can make the users suffer from impotency, miscarriage, birth of deformed babies, etc.\n"
            "⦿ Strained family and social relationships: It can lead to strained relationships with parents, friends and other people in the society. That is, parents as well as other people will reject them and regard them as bad influence on innocent people in the society.\n"
            "⦿ Legal prosecution and imprisonment risk: The use of substances such as marijuana, cocaine, etc. is described as illegal in the laws of Ghana. This means that the users face the risks of arrest, prosecution and imprisonment if they are reported to police.",
        explanation: ".",
      ),

      PracticeQuestion(
        scenario: "Question 2: (a) What four reasons do students give for abusing drugs?\n"
            "(b) State three defensive skills you would use to protect yourself against substance abuse.\n"
            "(BECE 2007 Q6)",
        answer: "Correct Answer: (a) Answer:\n"
            "⦿ Refusal to stick to medical advice: Attempting self-medication instead of getting a proper hospital prescription.\n"
            "⦿ Managing emotional stress: Trying to control feelings of shyness, anxiety, or anger.\n"
            "⦿ Ignorance of consequences: Not knowing the long-term harms or negative physical and mental outcomes.\n"
            "⦿ Peer pressure: Submitting to the influence or social expectations of friends who use drugs.\n"
            "⦿ Escaping economic stress: Using substances to temporarily forget systemic hardships like extreme poverty or domestic problems.\n"
            "⦿ Lack of academic self-control or curiosity: Wanting to experiment with altered states or trying to force long night study hours.\n\n"
            "Answer: (b)\n"
            "⦿ Firm refusal: Cultivating the personal habit of strictly saying \"No\" to drug abuse and staying completely away from illegal substances.\n"
            "⦿ Following legitimate medical prescriptions: Adhering strictly to clinical instructions on how legal medications must be taken.\n"
            "⦿ Seeking professional guidance: Actively utilizing counseling services from reliable authorities when facing stress or social challenges.\n"
            "⦿ Practising moral values: Implementing the ethical and clean living instructions taught within one's religious faith.\n"
            "⦿ Avoiding suspicious settings: Declining food, drinks, or gifts offered by suspicious individuals, known users, or bad peers.\n"
            "⦿ Effective time planning: Drafting a functional personal timetable for daily studies and maintaining structural discipline.\n"
            "⦿ Prioritising medical approval: Consulting a certified doctor before taking any health remedies to avoid self-medication bottlenecks.",
        explanation: ".",
      ),

      PracticeQuestion(
        scenario: "Question 3: (a) State three dangers associated with self-medication.\n"
            "(b) Suggest four ways of checking trade in drugs.\n"
            "(BECE 2015 Q6)",
        answer: "Correct Answer: (a) Answer:\n"
            "⦿ Increased potential for criminal behavior: It can break down standard moral inhibitions and lead to illegal actions such as theft or rebellion against school and parental authority.\n"
            "⦿ Psychological dependency and instability: The individual can manifest severe psychological instability or go through drug withdrawal or severe abstinence crises.\n"
            "⦿ Fatal consequences: Self-medication carries a highly critical risk of accidental overdose, which can immediately cause death.\n"
            "⦿ Loss of social networks and trust: It erodes your functional social credit, causing friends and community members to lose absolute trust in you.\n"
            "⦿ Mental disorders and erratic mood alterations: It directly induces critical shifts in mood, psychological clarity, and can eventually degenerate into full madness.\n"
            "⦿ Disruption of natural biological patterns: It severely alters natural circadian sleep rhythms, leading to systematic physical exhaustion.\n"
            "⦿ Irreversible somatic deformaties: It triggers complete dependency and can physically damage or permanently deform bodily systems and internal organs.\n\n"
            "Answer: (b)\n"
            "⦿ Strict anti-advertising legislation: Imposing powerful government bans on media channels against any open drug marketing or unauthorized herbal/chemical adverts.\n"
            "⦿ International tracking and collaboration: Forging tight borders and international partnerships to strictly track and monitor international chemical distributions.\n"
            "⦿ Aggressive enforcement at the source: Directly utilizing law enforcement forces like the Ghana Police Service and the Narcotics Control Board to track down and systematically dismantle the primary hubs of drug supply.\n"
            "⦿ Sustained media awareness crusades: Directing mainstream media networks to launch consistent informational programs outlining the structural risks of drug usage.\n"
            "⦿ Active parental surveillance: Prompting parents to constantly observe changes in their children's behavior and guide them away from negative peer groups.\n"
            "⦿ Peer-focused educational seminars: Instituting peer-led counseling groups within schools to build student solidarity against dangerous substances.\n"
            "⦿ Community whistleblowing actions: Encouraging community members to confidently volunteer tips to tracking agencies regarding neighborhood drug dealers.\n"
            "⦿ Severe legal penalties for dealers: Enacting heavy, uncompromised punitive prison sentences for illicit drug traders to serve as a public deterrent.",
        explanation: ".",
      ),
      PracticeQuestion(
        scenario: "Question 4: (a) Define the term decency.\n"
            "(b) Describe four behaviours considered as decent in your community.\n"
            "(c) Give three reasons for leading decent life in your community.\n"
            "(BECE 2016 Q6)",
        answer: "Correct Answer: (a) Answer:\n"
            "⦿ Decency is defined as a behavioral quality, attitude, or general lifestyle that fully conforms to the recognized and acceptable ethical standards of what is right, honest, and proper within a given society.\n\n"
            "Answer: (b)\n"
            "⦿ Respectful manual patterns: Choosing to never use the left hand when communicating, handing over objects, or greeting others.\n"
            "⦿ Academic and social integrity: Avoiding all forms of deceitful actions, such as cheating during school examination sessions.\n"
            "⦿ Communal care and kindness: Actively showing open love, kindness, hospitality, and practical assistance to fellow citizens.\n"
            "⦿ Owning up to faults: Publicly demonstrating sincere regret and taking responsibility for your wrong actions or mistakes.\n"
            "⦿ Chaste cross-gender interactions: Maintaining decent, disciplined, and morally upright interactions with peers of the opposite sex.\n"
            "⦿ Emotional self-mastery: Exercising strong emotional restraint and managing one's temper, even when under heavy provocation.\n"
